Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Excerpt:
"Company Material Adverse Effect" means any event, change, error, or circumstance that, whether considered singly or in combination with other events:
   (a) has caused or would reasonably be expected to cause a material adverse impact on the overall business, assets, liabilities, or financial condition of the Company; provided that for purposes of clause (a), none of the following shall be deemed to constitute an adverse impact: 
       (i) standard fluctuations in currency or interest rates;
       (ii) broad economic slowdowns or market-wide downturns affecting comparable industry participants;
       (iii) regulatory or legislative modifications, except where such changes have a substantially disproportionate effect on the Company relative to its peers;
       (iv) force majeure events such as natural disasters or pandemics, insofar as they reflect general market conditions;
   OR
   (b) would, or would reasonably be expected to, prevent, materially delay, or materially impair the Company’s ability to consummate the Merger. Importantly, no exceptions or carveouts—whether related to accounting standard changes, political shifts, or other external factors—shall be applied to events described under clause (b). (Pages 12-14)
